			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842422">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I like the idea of presenting this as like&#8230; vast sea creature that feeds off humans and lives in the depths and like&#8230;as if it's something to be scared of before the ending reveals they're actually looking for love; but something about the way this is done feels&#8230;iffy?</p>
<p>I think it's because the SCP just, immediately tells us with no real build up or anything; it's just, passive story being told and then ending 'oh yeah i feel in love' and it feels like it needs&#8230;more showing? Like, there's a version of this I can see where you completely strip out the interview logs and add a short addendum that implies a few details that in turn imply the love angle where it feels like this might hit harder; or alternatively taking the love angle and actually running with it, exploring the kinda&#8230;sadness of the angler fish as they make someone they love part of themselves but in turn, slowly lose everything about the person that they fell in love with, y'know? Really play with this in a deeply emotional way.</p>
<p>Part of me wonders if the description could be trimmed down a lot: like, there's some big paragraphs about the mating behaviour of these things and I wonder whether there's a better way of conveying them than dense prose; perhaps just leaving it somewhat implied and only covering it in very broad strokes? Same goes for a lot of the details about -1: I wonder whether there's a way to nail the emotions here without spending so much time setting everything up exactly?</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Coastal regions most frequented by SCP-X are to monitored weekly by local marine vessels equipped with sonar capable of bathymetric mapping.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>the 'most frequented' here feels clunky; perhaps just cutting the most would help? It might be that 'SCP-X' is read as a singular thing and so adding 'instances' after to emphasise that there's a whole bunch of them would help?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-X-1 and any other captured instances are to held at Site-66 in a saltwater tank set to mimic ocean</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd slap commas are 'and any other capture instances' so this flows better</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The tank is to undergo monthly cleaning and upkeep and is to coincide with a feeding schedule consisting of 1200kg of assorted bony fish and crustaceans.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not really sure the part about 'monthly cleaning and upkeep' is necessary, and 'to coincide with a feeding schedule' feels awkward here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to SCP-X’s anomalous abilities all maintenance, feedings, and interviews are to be conducted solely by female staff.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>add a comma after abilities</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-X is the collective designation assigned to large anomalous instances of Lophiiformes, or the deep sea anglerfish.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'large' feels vague here; there's an implication this might be meant to be e.g. 'large number of&#8230;' as opposed to 'large sized' but? Really my concern is just that 'large' and 'anomalous' doesn't actually tell us anything about the SCP.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In addition, all instances possess an exceptionally long illicium or fishing rod, capable of extending at least 100m.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'fishing rod' doesn't quite clarify what an illicium is enough: I'd suggest cutting that and adding a footnote instead explaining illicium in more words.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In place of the esca or lure, the illicium terminates into a realistic and anatomically accurate body of a human female, connected at the base of the skull.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Same here; or alternatively using &quot;In place of the esca, or lore, the&#8230;&quot;</p>
<p>'terminates in a realistic' might be clearer but?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>reports of an enormous beached fish near the boardwalks of ███████, ██████ reached imbedded agents within the local police department.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why is this redacted? In universe, it seems like the location would be important context for researchers, and out of universe, it's an irrelevant detail that is made to seem important via the use of redaction.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and <span style="color: green">it</span> currently resides in the marine wing of Site-66.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>(SCP-X-1 has been allowed to remove its lure from its tank to speak directly to the interviewer.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>IMO: contextualise this more; briefly explain <em>why</em> it's being interviewed.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. ██████</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Give this dude a name. It'll make this section much clearer.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
</div>
</div>

